{
 "id": 121,
 "created_at": "2026-10-26T01:34:46.199857+05:30",
 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DbzXRy1MthY/",
 "title": "Introduce Your Price, Not Your Power",
 "topic": "mindset",
 "summary": "The creator explains that in networking and social situations, people should introduce themselves by their price, limits, values, and ideals rather than trying to impress others with their power. She claims that coming in with clear standards attracts the right support and community.",
 "key_points": [
  "Never introduce yourself by your power; always introduce yourself by your price.",
  "People often walk into networking events acting like they are auditioning.",
  "Coming in with your limits, boundaries, values, and ideals is important because that is what people reject if they do not match your worth.",
  "Do not lower your standards to build a community, as a community is built off of standards.",
  "The creator claims that the more you look like you are helping yourself, the more you'll be offered help."
 ],
 "content": "Power Rule #2 Introduce Your Price, Not Your Power. Never introduce yourself by your power. Always introduce yourself by your price. When you go into a networking event, people have the tendency to walk in like they're auditioning. One of my mistakes is, I think we're going to film an episode of Friends. I've never really approached networking beyond just trying to meet people and hang out, and and that has helped me more than what you might think, because especially in these times, it's not easy to find help. And it's especially not easy to find help if you've been raised hyper independent and never really had a support system, including now at this point of your life. And I think that triggers people to think they have to kind of earn everyone and not come in with their limits, their boundaries, their values, their ideals as much as their ideas, because that is what people reject. Not matching with you, not feeling you are worth your value. And that can make someone feel automatically abandoned, like they should lower their standard so that they can build a community. But a community is built off of standards. And even though you've been alone a lot of your life, abandoned, left to figure things out on your own, and I'm sorry for that, kind of have to accept that energy as a part of life, not just your life, in order to find the community and build the community that won't allow it to happen to you. The grand irony of life is, my narcissistic dad taught me this. The more you look like you're helping yourself, the more you'll be offered help. Instagram reel/post by Diandra. Text / caption: Power Rules By Daddy Recovery #daddyrecovery #podcast #power #empower #lifecoach",
